  (W, "Passphrase protection is the encryption of a private key under a passphrase, a secret phrase chosen by the owner, so that the key can be stored or printed without being usable by whoever finds it. BIP38 specifies how: it proposes a method of encrypting and encoding a passphrase-protected private key record as a 58-character base58check string [B38]. The record contains everything needed to reconstitute the private key except the passphrase."),
  (Y, "The proposal was written for paper wallets and physical bitcoins. Its motivation is a two-factor arrangement, something you have plus something you know: a person holding the printed record cannot spend without the passphrase, and the passphrase can be sent through another channel, for instance the telephone, while the record goes by post [B38]. The third edition of the book does not cover the format at all, whereas the course material that accompanies this chapter, written on the second edition, presents it: there BIP38 is described as a common standard for encrypting private keys with a passphrase and encoding them with base58check, so that they can be stored on backup media or transported between wallets, with the Advanced Encryption Standard named as the cipher [AL]."),
  (E, "The BIPs repository gives BIP38 the status Deployed, yet the summary of the comments on it reads Unanimously Discourage for implementation [B38]. The two statements are compatible: the format exists and is in use, and those who commented advise against building it into new software. The format is therefore of interest for old paper wallets and encrypted keys that someone may still hold."),
  (H, "The encryption uses three tools. The passphrase is stretched into a key by scrypt, a key derivation function that is deliberately slow and memory-hungry in order to resist brute-force attacks. The 32-byte private key is then encrypted with AES, a widely used block cipher, in two halves of 16 bytes. The first four bytes of the double SHA256 of the Bitcoin address serve as a salt for scrypt and as a check that the passphrase was right [B38]. The result is encoded in base58check with the 2-byte prefix 0x0142 for a record without EC multiplication, followed by 37 further bytes; the specification publishes the first and the last of the 2 to the 296 possible strings of each kind, and the chapter's checks reproduce the first, 6PRHv1jg1ytiE4kT2QtrUz8gEjMQghZDWg1FuxjdYDzjUkcJeGdFj9q9Vi, from the bytes 01 42 C0 and thirty-six zeros with the base58check procedure of the toolkit."),
