  (W, "A deterministic wallet is a wallet whose private keys are all derived from a single random value, called the seed, by a procedure that always gives the same results for the same seed. The book of this course shows the idea with a hash function: if the seed is hashed together with the numbers 0, 1 and 2, three seemingly random values come out, and using the same hash function with the same seed always gives the same values [BK]. The book says that a user of deterministic key generation can back up every key in the wallet by simply recording the seed and a reference to the algorithm used [BK]."),
  (Y, "The earlier arrangement, in which a wallet generated independent keys, needed a new backup every time a new key was generated or imported. The fourth chapter's section on key formats says that later wallets began to use deterministic wallets, which only ever need to be backed up once for typical use on the blockchain [BK]. This is also why Chapter 1's recovery code can be a short list of words: it encodes the seed from which everything else follows."),
  (E, "Almost all current wallets are of this type. The consequences for this chapter are the ones that the book draws: a single exported key can endanger the others, keys cannot be imported, and a vanity address, which comes from a separate search, does not fit into the scheme unless the user backs up additional data for it [BK]. The more elaborate hierarchical form, with a tree of keys derived from one seed, is the subject of the next chapter."),
