  (W, "Address reuse is the practice of receiving more than one payment to the same address. Because all the transactions are public, every payment to an address is visible together with every other payment to it. The book makes the point about vanity addresses: using a vanity address to receive multiple payments to the same address creates a link between all of those payments [BK]."),
  (Y, "The link matters for people other than the receiver. The book gives two small stories: Alice may want to donate anonymously, and Bob may not want his other customers to know that he gives discount pricing to the charity [BK]. The same reasoning stands behind the first design of Bitcoin, in which the receiver's node supplied a new public key that it had never previously given anyone, so that different payments to Bob could not be connected by someone looking at the blockchain [BK]."),
