  (W, "A paper wallet is a private key printed on paper, often together with the corresponding Bitcoin address for convenience, though this is not necessary, because the address can be derived from the private key [BK]. The book shows a sample and describes many designs: some for gifts, with seasonal themes; some for storage in a bank vault with the key hidden by a scratch-off sticker or by tamper-proof foil; some with detachable stubs that allow several copies to be kept against fire or flood [BK]."),
  (Y, "A wallet on paper was an answer to the question of how to keep a key away from computers that can be attacked. The book now declares the answer wrong. Paper wallets are an obsolete technology and dangerous for most users, there are many subtle pitfalls in generating them, not least that the generating code might be compromised with a back door, and many bitcoins have been stolen in this way; they are shown for informational purposes only and should not be used for storing bitcoin [BK]."),
